    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Aquarium-Technician_JR-019108-1": {
      "title": "Aquarium Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Continuing  Department: ESG – Melbourne Bioresources - COO Portfolio  Salary: UOM 3 - $69,554 - $76,795 p.a. plus 17% super  Work in a state-of-the-art zebrafish research facility / support world-class scientific discovery / develop specialist aquaculture and animal care skills  Join a dedicated animal care team and apply your aquaculture skills to support impactful research.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity:   In this Aquarium Technician role, you will ensure the health and welfare of zebrafish that underpin cutting-edge research. You will develop specialised aquaculture and laboratory animal skills while working in a highly collaborative, research-focused environment.  What you will deliver:   Provide daily husbandry, feeding, cleaning and health monitoring of zebrafish to the highest animal welfare standards.  Perform routine tank cleaning, facility hygiene tasks and basic equipment care to ensure reliable aquarium operations.  Conduct regular water quality testing, record results accurately and report issues promptly to support stable system performance.  Maintain breeding colonies and live food cultures to support research requirements and colony health.  Adhere to regulatory and ethical requirements, including AWV, POCTA, OGTR and DAFF, in all facility activities.  You may be a great fit if you:   Hold completed Year 12 and a Certificate II in aquaculture, or equivalent relevant experience and training.  Demonstrate strong attention to detail, clear communication skills and reliable record-keeping in technical or animal care settings.  Apply sound organisational skills to manage routine tasks efficiently and follow established standard operating procedures independently.  Collaborate effectively within a small technical team to support consistent facility operations and animal welfare outcomes.  Bring experience with recirculating aquaculture systems, equipment maintenance or aquatic animal health monitoring, or similar environments.  Offer prior experience working in a laboratory animal facility or regulated animal research environment.  Are available to undertake occasional weekend work as required to support facility operations.  What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team:   Within the Chief Operating Officer Portfolio, you will join the Melbourne Bioresources platform in the state-of-the-art Zebrafish Aquarium. The team enables high-impact research by providing exceptional animal care, technical support and reliable aquatic systems for University of Melbourne researchers. You will be part of a collaborative, detail-focused group that values animal welfare, continuous improvement and developing specialist skills in aquaculture and laboratory animal science.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application.   Want to know more?   For questions related to our recruitment process email Alessandro Storani at Alessandro.storani@unimelb.edu.au    For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.  Applications close: ​ Sunday 25 October 2026 - 11:55 PM ; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:   PD_JR-019108 Aquarium Technician .docx  PD_JR-019108 Aquarium Technician .pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Assistant-to-Head-of-Department_JR-019137": {
      "title": "Research Assistant to Head of Department",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nRole type:  Part-time/ Full Time; 0.5 – 1 FTE; Fixed-term for 5 months Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department/ School: Department of Medical Education, Melbourne Medical School Salary: Level A: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. pro rata for part time plus 17% super Work alongside a senior Head of Department / deepen qualitative and quantitative research expertise / contribute to medical education impact Apply your research skills in medical education and grow your academic profile in a supportive team Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will work closely with the Head of Department on high-quality medical education research projects. You will strengthen your qualitative and quantitative analysis skills while contributing to publications, presentations and curriculum innovation. What you will deliver:   Provide research and administrative support to the Head of Department across multiple medical education projects. Conduct qualitative analyses and support quantitative analyses of administrative, longitudinal and linked assessment datasets. Manage, assemble and clean datasets, ensuring data quality for reporting, manuscripts and conference presentations. Coordinate ethics applications and processes, and maintain productive relationships with project partners and stakeholders. Conduct literature searches and systematic reviews, and contribute to written reports, papers and presentations.   You may be a great fit if you:   Hold an undergraduate degree in a relevant discipline and an interest in medical education research. Use qualitative analysis software such as NVivo and manage survey data using platforms like Qualtrics. Demonstrate strong skills in literature review, synthesis of peer-reviewed and grey literature, and academic writing. Understand human research ethics principles and have experience preparing and managing ethics submissions. Bring excellent attention to detail, diligence and organisational skills to manage concurrent research tasks and deadlines. Value collaborative work in multidisciplinary teams and communicate effectively with diverse colleagues and stakeholders. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – You will join the Department of Medical Education within the Melbourne Medical School at Parkville. The Department leads the Doctor of Medicine curriculum, postgraduate programmes and research that improves health professional education. Research spans work integrated learning, curriculum and assessment, healthcare communication and student engagement. You will collaborate with academics, clinicians and professional staff in a supportive, values-driven environment encouraging innovation. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Anna Ryan by emailing annatr@unimelb.edu.au. For questions related to our recruitment process email Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 29 October 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-019137 Research Assistant to Head of Department PD.docx JR-019137 Research Assistant to Head of Department PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Research-Assistant_JR-018720": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Sleep and Daily Rhythms",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed-term (1 year) Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department: Centre for Youth Mental Health Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super Work in a world-leading youth mental health centre / drive NHMRC-funded LUMOS sleep–circadian research / shape early psychosis detection and care Apply now to support young people at risk of psychosis through rigorous sleep and circadian research Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne  We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity In this role, you will support the LUMOS study, tracking sleep and circadian rhythms in youth psychosis. You will gain hands-on experience in clinical interviewing, neurocognitive assessment and high-quality longitudinal research. You will work within a multidisciplinary team at Orygen, translating discoveries into better early intervention decisions. Occasional off-site (e.g. at Orygen headspace centres) visits to participants, regular attendance at the study sites, and some out of hours work will be required. What you will deliver: Recruit and enrol young participants into the LUMOS sleep and circadian study across clinical services. Conduct structured clinical interviews and neurocognitive assessments, including pupil measures and computerised cognitive tasks. Maintain accurate, confidential, audit-ready participant records and study databases in line with Good Clinical Practice guidelines. Liaise with clinical teams, young people and families to support recruitment, follow-up and engagement over 12 months. Assist with ethics and governance submissions, standard operating procedures, manuscripts and presentations for multidisciplinary project meetings. You may be a great fit if you: Hold an honours-level degree in behavioural or social sciences, or equivalent education and relevant experience. Demonstrate strong interest in youth mental health and sound understanding of psychiatric diagnoses and disorders. Conduct structured interviews and engage sensitively with young people experiencing mental health difficulties. Follow complex research protocols, maintain precise records and troubleshoot issues in fast-paced clinical research settings. Use electronic data capture platforms, preferably REDCap, for secure entry, verification and basic data analysis. Manage workloads independently across multiple sites, collaborating with multidisciplinary stakeholders and meeting competing deadlines. For further information please refer to the attached PD. What we offer you! We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including: Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program For more information check out our benefits page! Your new team – Centre for Youth Mental Health At the Centre for Youth Mental Health, you will join a pioneering leader in youth mental health research. Orygen’s multidisciplinary teams integrate clinical services, research and knowledge translation to improve outcomes for young people. You will work alongside clinicians, scientists and allied professionals within Australia’s largest health sciences faculty. The team values curiosity, rigour, collaboration and genuine impact on early intervention and prevention in psychosis. Be Yourself The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process . Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria , found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Dr Mag de Rozario at mag.derozario@unimelb.edu.au . For questions related to our recruitment process email Vanessa Phan at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 29 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone. Position Description: PD_JR-018720 Research Assistant in Sleep and Daily Rhythms.docx PD_JR-018720 Research Assistant in Sleep and Daily Rhythms.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/The-Director--Enterprise-Partnerships--Health-and-Life-Sciences-_JR-019078": {
      "title": "Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health and Life Sciences)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-14\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDirector, Enterprise Partnerships (Health and Life Sciences) Melbourne Enterprise | Melbourne Research and Enterprise Portfolio Full-time | Fixed term for 5 years The University of Melbourne is home to some of Australia's most significant research, innovation and entrepreneurial capabilities, with a track record that includes strategic agreements with leading global organisations. This is a significant opportunity to build on that foundation, grow the University's external partnerships and unlock new opportunities across the health and life sciences sector. We are seeking an exceptional senior leader to lead Enterprise Partnerships across health and life sciences. Reporting to the Executive Director, Partnerships and Precincts, you will identify, shape and convert strategic opportunities that connect the University's world-class capabilities with industry, government, health services, medical research institutes and other key partners. As a senior institutional interface, you will bring together expertise from across the University to develop high-impact partnerships that accelerate research translation, support improved health outcomes, build workforce capability, influence policy and generate lasting economic and societal impact. The opportunity This is a career-defining opportunity to build on a strong foundation of global partnerships and shape the next generation of health and life sciences engagement at one of the world's leading universities. Building on a strong track record of strategic agreements that are among the first of their kind in Australia, you will have the opportunity to develop new partnerships and extend the University's impact across Australia and internationally. You will lead strategic partnership development, business development and key account management across the sector, building trusted relationships with senior external stakeholders and translating the University's research, education, talent and innovation capabilities into compelling partner propositions. You will also strengthen a more market-facing, sector-led and partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, while leading and developing a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. Key responsibilities include: Originate, shape and convert high-value strategic partnerships, collaborations and commercial opportunities across the health and life sciences sector. Build influential relationships with senior leaders across industry, government, healthcare, medical research and innovation, both nationally and internationally. Translate the University's research, education, talent, commercialisation and enterprise capabilities into compelling value propositions for partners. Identify and prioritise a strong pipeline of opportunities, applying commercial judgement, governance and disciplined account and performance management. Mobilise multidisciplinary expertise across faculties, research institutes and professional services to respond to complex opportunities and maximise impact. Strengthen a sector-led, partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, including data-informed decision-making and effective use of CRM and emerging technologies. Lead and develop a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. About you You are an accomplished partnerships, commercial, business development or enterprise leader with deep knowledge of the health and life sciences sector and a strong track record of creating and growing complex, high-value partnerships. You bring strategic insight, commercial acumen and exceptional relationship-building capability, with the credibility to engage senior stakeholders, navigate complex environments and turn opportunities into meaningful outcomes. You will bring: Significant experience leading strategic partnerships, business development or commercial activity within industry, government, healthcare, research or innovation environments. Deep knowledge of the health and life sciences sector, with the ability to identify emerging trends across biotechnology, medical technologies, pharmaceuticals, precision medicine, research translation and related fields and translate these into strategic partnership opportunities. Experience working within, or closely alongside, research-intensive, innovation-led or knowledge-based organisations, with the ability to connect complex research capability with external partner needs. A strong track record of establishing and growing influential relationships across industry, government, healthcare, research and innovation ecosystems and converting these into strategic opportunities. Demonstrated commercial judgement in structuring complex partnerships, evaluating opportunities, managing risk and navigating governance to achieve sustainable outcomes. Experience shaping new approaches, capabilities or operating models in complex environments and bringing diverse stakeholders with you. Outstanding communication, influencing and stakeholder engagement skills, with the judgement and credibility to build alignment and influence senior decision-makers. A collaborative and inclusive leadership style, with experience setting direction, building capability and developing high-performing teams. How to apply To apply, please submit: A current CV. A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your suitability for the role. For role or application process enquiries, please contact Lucy Porter , Executive Recruitment Partner, at exec-recruitment@unimelb.edu.au . If you are ready to build on world-class research and innovation capabilities, grow high-impact partnerships and shape lasting health, economic and societal impact, we invite you to apply. Applications must be submitted through the online application portal and will not be accepted by email. Please note: The University is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Applications close: Monday 9th November 2026 at 11:55pm Melbourne time.   Position Description: PD - Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health, LS).pdf PD - Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Health, LS).docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Director--Partnership-Success-Group_JR-019079": {
      "title": "Director, Partnership Success Group",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-14\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDirector, Partnership Success Group Melbourne Enterprise | Melbourne Research and Enterprise Portfolio Full-time | Fixed term for 5 years The University of Melbourne is one of the world's leading research-intensive universities, with the research capability, talent and enterprise ambition to solve society's most significant challenges. Melbourne Enterprise is building a more coordinated, market-facing and partner-centric institution, connecting world-class research and expertise with industry, government and other external partners. We are seeking an exceptional senior leader to lead the Partnership Success Group, a pivotal enterprise capability that will shape how the University mobilises, delivers and deepens partnerships across research, innovation and workforce development. Reporting to the Executive Director, Partnerships and Precincts, you will bring together commercial development, market intelligence and systems, partnership mobilisation and delivery, workforce capability and organisational capability development to enable partnership growth at scale. As a key institutional leader, you will work across faculties, Melbourne Enterprise and professional services to mobilise expertise and resources around complex opportunities, strengthen commercial and delivery capability, and create a consistently exceptional partner experience. You will play a central role in establishing the systems, frameworks, governance and ways of working that enable a consistent, market-facing and partner-centric approach to partnering across the University. The opportunity This is a career-defining opportunity to build enterprise capability that will shape how one of the world's leading universities develops, mobilises and delivers partnerships at scale. You will lead the Partnership Success Group, strengthening the University's ability to shape opportunities, structure partnerships and deliver them effectively. Working closely with sector-aligned Enterprise Partnerships leaders and colleagues across the University, you will bring together commercial, delivery and institutional capability to turn major opportunities into enduring partnerships and measurable impact. You will also lead the University's workforce capability partnering function, bringing together professional education, custom education and industry talent solutions into a coordinated enterprise offering that connects partners with the University's education, workforce and talent capabilities, including students, PhDs and graduates. A key focus will be building the systems, intelligence and organisational capability that enable better decision-making, effective prioritisation, strong partnership performance and an exceptional experience for partners. Key responsibilities include: Establish and lead the Partnership Success Group, building the commercial, delivery and organisational capability required to mobilise and deliver partnerships at scale. Lead the University's workforce capability partnering function, aligning professional education, custom education and industry talent solutions with partner needs and institutional priorities. Provide strategic leadership for partnership mobilisation and delivery, ensuring major opportunities and partnerships are governed effectively, executed consistently and deliver value for partners and the University. Lead the uplift of partnership development capability across research, innovation and workforce development, including opportunity shaping, business case development, partnership structuring and commercial frameworks. Establish and enhance partner intelligence, CRM, pipeline management and reporting capabilities to support effective prioritisation, resource allocation and decision-making. Mobilise cross-functional teams and institutional resources around complex opportunities, working across faculties and professional services to maximise partnership outcomes. Establish best-practice frameworks, standards and tools that embed consistent ways of working and build enterprise-wide partnership capability. About you You are an accomplished partnerships, commercial, business development or enterprise transformation leader with a strong track record of building capability, leading complex functions and converting opportunities into sustainable organisational, commercial or societal outcomes. You bring strategic insight, commercial acumen and exceptional stakeholder engagement capability, with the credibility to work across a complex, matrixed organisation and influence senior executives, academic leaders, professional teams and external partners. You will bring: Significant experience leading strategic growth, business development, partnership delivery or enterprise transformation functions, with a track record of converting opportunities into sustainable outcomes. Demonstrated ability to mobilise diverse functions around large-scale, complex and high-value opportunities, bringing together commercial, delivery, technical and stakeholder capability to achieve institutional outcomes. Strong technology-enabled leadership, championing digital innovation, human-centred design and data-driven approaches to partner engagement, pipeline management and growth. An ability to create the conditions and environment for innovation. Strong commercial acumen, including experience developing business cases, shaping partnership models, structuring opportunities and navigating financial, governance, risk and delivery considerations. Proven experience establishing, leading or transforming complex functions, operating models or organisational capabilities, including embedding new ways of working and leading organisational change. Exceptional stakeholder engagement, influencing and negotiation skills, with the ability to build credibility and trust with senior executives, academic leaders, professional teams and external partners. Highly developed strategic judgement and problem-solving skills, with the agility to operate in ambiguity, balance competing priorities and make sound decisions at pace. A collaborative and influential leadership style, with the ability to break down organisational silos, align diverse stakeholders around shared outcomes and build trusted relationships across the University. How to apply To apply, please submit: A current CV. A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your suitability for the role. For role or application process enquiries, please contact Lucy Porter , Executive Recruitment Partner, at exec-recruitment@unimelb.edu.au . If you are ready to build enterprise capability that will shape how the University develops, delivers and deepens partnerships at scale, we invite you to apply. Applications must be submitted through the online application portal and will not be accepted by email. Please note: The University is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Applications close: Monday 2nd November 2026 at 11:55pm Melbourne time. Position Description: PD - JR-019079 Director, Partnership Success Group.pdf PD - JR-019079 Director, Partnership Success Group.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Deputy-Director-of-Indigenous-Medical-Education_JR-018456-1": {
      "title": "Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-14\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type:  Full Time; Continuing Faculty: Medicine, Dentistry and Health Sciences Department: Department of Medical Education Salary: Level B $124,656 - $148,023 p.a. plus 17% super  Shape Indigenous medical education across Melbourne’s Doctor of Medicine program through curriculum innovation, meaningful partnerships and culturally safe practice to improve First Nations health outcomes. Apply now to drive strategic curriculum leadership, evidence-informed teaching and meaningful community engagement in one of Australia’s leading medical schools. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as these positions are exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic).  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   Help shape the next generation of doctors through leadership in Indigenous medical education. You will guide curriculum innovation, assessment and community engagement initiatives that advance culturally safe practice and contribute to improved First Nations health outcomes. What you will deliver:   Provide leadership for the development and delivery of First Nations health education across the Doctor of Medicine program, ensuring alignment with learning outcomes, curriculum priorities and accreditation requirements. Drive the design and continuous improvement of culturally safe, evidence-informed learning and assessment practices, including innovative teaching resources, placements and digital learning experiences. Support and enable high-performing teams and partnerships, fostering collaboration across academic, clinical and community settings to achieve impactful educational outcomes. Strengthen engagement with First Nations communities and health organisations, ensuring Indigenous knowledges, perspectives and priorities inform curriculum, educational practice and quality improvement activities. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a tertiary qualification in health, education, public health or an equivalent combination of training. Bring experience in health professional education, with expertise in curriculum design, assessment and educational quality improvement in a tertiary or healthcare setting. Demonstrate leadership and collaboration skills, with the ability to influence practice, support teams and work effectively across complex organisational environments. Build strong and respectful relationships with diverse stakeholders, including First Nations communities, health services, academic colleagues and partner organisations. Have a strong understanding of First Nations health, cultural safety and health equity, together with a commitment to improving outcomes for First Nations peoples through education. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – “The Wurru Wurru Health Unit”   At the Wurru Wurru Health Unit, we are shaping the future of medical education. Through partnerships with First Nations communities, clinicians and educators we lead the design and delivery of First Nations health education that prepares future doctors to provide culturally safe, responsive and equitable care. As part of our team, you will have the opportunity to influence one of Australia's leading medical programs, contribute to sector-leading curriculum innovation, and work alongside passionate colleagues dedicated to advancing First Nations health and educational excellence. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the our recruitment process, including application tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A valid Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please submit: A current resume (CV) A cover letter outlining your interest in the role and relevant experience Your responses to the selection criteria, found in the position description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Dr Bridie Hofstee at b.hofstee@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process, contact Walid at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments, contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 8 November 2026 11:55 PM; AEDT.  Position Description:   JR-018456 Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education PD.docx JR-018456 Deputy Director of Indigenous Medical Education PD.pdf"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Director--Enterprise-Partnerships--Energy--Primary-Industries---Education-_JR-019080": {
      "title": "Director, Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries & Education)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-14\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nDirector, Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries & Education) Melbourne Enterprise | Melbourne Research and Enterprise Portfolio Continuing | Fixed-term for 5 years The University of Melbourne is home to some of Australia's most significant research, innovation and entrepreneurial capabilities, with a track record that includes strategic agreements with leading global organisations. This is a significant opportunity to build on that foundation, grow the University's external partnerships and unlock new opportunities across energy, primary industries and education. We are seeking an exceptional senior leader to lead Enterprise Partnerships across energy, primary industries and education. Reporting to the Executive Director, Partnerships and Precincts, you will identify, shape and convert strategic opportunities that connect the University's world-class capabilities with industry, government, primary industries, education providers and other key partners. As a senior institutional interface, you will bring together expertise from across the University to develop high-impact partnerships that accelerate the energy transition, strengthen sustainable and productive primary industries, build workforce capability, influence policy and generate lasting economic and societal impact. The opportunity This is a career-defining opportunity to build on a strong foundation of global partnerships and shape the next generation of engagement across energy, primary industries and education at one of the world's leading universities. Building on a strong track record of strategic agreements that are among the first of their kind in Australia, you will have the opportunity to develop new partnerships and extend the University's impact across Australia and internationally. You will lead strategic partnership development, business development and key account management across the sectors, building trusted relationships with senior external stakeholders and translating the University's research, education, talent and innovation capabilities into compelling partner propositions. You will also strengthen a more market-facing, sector-led and partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, while leading and developing a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. Key responsibilities include: Originate, shape and convert high-value strategic partnerships, collaborations and commercial opportunities across energy, primary industries and education. Build influential relationships with senior leaders across industry, government, research, education, primary industries and innovation, both nationally and internationally. Translate the University's research, education, talent, commercialisation and enterprise capabilities into compelling value propositions for partners. Identify and prioritise a strong pipeline of opportunities, applying commercial judgement, governance and disciplined account and performance management. Mobilise multidisciplinary expertise across faculties, research institutes and professional services to respond to complex opportunities and maximise impact. Strengthen a sector-led, partner-centric approach to enterprise engagement, including data-informed decision-making and effective use of CRM and emerging technologies. Lead and develop a high-performing partnerships team aligned to the University's strategic priorities. About you You are an accomplished partnerships, commercial, business development or enterprise leader with deep knowledge of the energy, primary industries and/or education sectors and a strong track record of creating and growing complex, high-value partnerships. You bring strategic insight, commercial acumen and exceptional relationship-building capability, with the credibility to engage senior stakeholders, navigate complex environments and turn opportunities into meaningful outcomes. You will bring: Significant experience leading strategic partnerships, business development or commercial activity within industry, government, research, education, primary industries or innovation environments. Deep knowledge of the energy, primary industries and/or education landscape, with the ability to identify emerging trends across energy transition, climate resilience, biodiversity and environmental sustainability, sustainable agriculture and food systems, workforce development, educational innovation and lifelong learning, and translate these into strategic partnership opportunities. Experience working within, or closely alongside, research-intensive, innovation-led or knowledge-based organisations, with the ability to connect complex research capability with external partner needs. A strong track record of establishing and growing influential relationships across industry, government, research, education, sustainability and innovation ecosystems and converting these into strategic opportunities. Demonstrated commercial judgement in structuring complex partnerships, evaluating opportunities, managing risk and navigating governance to achieve sustainable outcomes. Experience shaping new approaches, capabilities or operating models in complex environments and bringing diverse stakeholders with you. Outstanding communication, influencing and stakeholder engagement skills, with the judgement and credibility to build alignment and influence senior decision-makers. A collaborative and inclusive leadership style, with experience setting direction, building capability and developing high-performing teams. How to apply To apply, please submit: A current CV. A cover letter (maximum two pages) outlining your suitability for the role. For role or application process enquiries, please contact Lucy Porter, Executive Recruitment Partner, at exec-recruitment@unimelb.edu.au . If you are ready to build on world-class research and innovation capabilities, grow high-impact partnerships and shape lasting environmental, economic, workforce and societal impact, we invite you to apply. Applications must be submitted through the online application portal and will not be accepted by email. Please note: The University is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Applications close: Monday 2nd November at 11:55pm Melbourne time. Position Description: PD - JR-019080 Director Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries, Education).pdf PD - JR-019080 Director Enterprise Partnerships (Energy, Primary Industries, Education).docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Brisbane-Queensland/Manager---Ecosystem-Partnerships---Engagement--QLD--NSW---ACT-_JR-018830": {
      "title": "Manager – Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (QLD, NSW & ACT)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nRole type: Part Time (0.6FTE); Fixed Term for 12 months Faculty: Faculty of Business & Economics Location : Brisbane, Queensland Department:  Dilin Duwa Centre for Indigenous Business Leadership Salary: $125,191 - $135,504 p.a. pro-rata for part time plus 17% super  Only Indigenous Australians are eligible to apply as this position is exempt under the Special Measure Provision, Section 12 (1) of the Equal Opportunity Act 2011 (Vic). Strategic Indigenous ecosystem leadership across QLD, NSW and ACT / community and partner engagement / grow impact through funded initiative Join Dilin Duwa to strengthen Indigenous business networks and lead high impact partnerships across QLD, NSW and ACT. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will lead ecosystem partnerships and engagement across eastern Australia, connecting Indigenous businesses, communities and partners. You will translate relationships into new programmes, initiatives and revenue that grow Dilin Duwa’s reach and impact. What you will deliver:   Lead regional engagement initiatives, workshops and dialogues that are culturally grounded and aligned with Dilin Duwa. Build and manage strategic partnerships across the Indigenous business ecosystem, including corporations, government, universities and philanthropy. Drive business development opportunities, developing proposals, funding submissions and enterprise services that expand contracted revenue. Strengthen alumni, leadership and regional networks of Indigenous entrepreneurs and organisations connected to Dilin Duwa. Oversee programme delivery and partnerships from planning through evaluation, monitoring outcomes and reporting insights for improvement. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a relevant postgraduate qualification or equivalent senior experience in a related field. Be of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent and bring lived experience to this work. Demonstrate strong experience working with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities or organisations. Build and sustain complex relationships and partnerships across community, corporate, government and ecosystem stakeholders. Manage programs and projects end-to-end, with strong organisational skills and high-level project management capability. Use Microsoft Office confidently and adapt quickly to new software, systems and digital engagement tools. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave and cultural leave Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team Dilin Duwa is a pioneering Centre for Indigenous Business Leadership within the Faculty of Business and Economics. The Centre partners with Melbourne Business School to deliver research, education and ecosystem services nationally. You will join a collaborative team committed to Indigenous economic independence and thriving Indigenous enterprises. The team values culturally grounded practice, deep community engagement and evidence based impact. Based in Brisbane, you will connect regional ecosystems with Dilin Duwa’s national networks and capabilities.  Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application. Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Paul Paton by emailing p.paton@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Nick Peterson at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: Tuesday 27 October 2026 at 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   Word : Manager - Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (QLD, NSW & ACT)_PD (1).docx PDF : Manager - Ecosystem Partnerships & Engagement (QLD, NSW & ACT)_PD (1).pdf"
