    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Graduate-Researcher-Academic-Associate-in-History-and-Theory_JR-019174": {
      "title": "Graduate Researcher Academic Associate in History and Theory",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nJob no: several new positions Location:  Parkville Role type:  0.2 FTE Part-time Fixed-term for 24-months, tied to candidature and valid work rights Faculty:  Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning Salary:  Level A.2 – $92,243 per annum, pro rata for part-time, plus 17% super   New opportunity to gain meaningful academic experience in education in the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning Undertake career advancing professional development, including 30 hours paid professional development Salary packaging, subsidised health and wellbeing services, fitness and cultural clubs, Myki discounts, and a 25% discount on graduate courses to our staff and their immediate families   About the Role The Faculty invites applications for several new Graduate Researcher Academic Associate (GRAA) positions from graduate researchers currently enrolled in a PhD in the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning with discipline and domain knowledge in the subjects comprising the Undergraduate Bachelor of Design with a focus in built environment history and theory taught in the Architecture Major.    As a GRAA in History and Theory you will contribute positively, both individually and as part of a team, to educating, inspiring and supporting students. The GRAAs will be responsible for carrying out education related activity for one or more subjects. Key education responsibilities will include: ·         Teaching, including through tutorials, seminars, workshops ·         Undertaking preparation and associated work, including teaching and student consultation ·         Regular consultation with students, monitor discussion boards/forums, respond to queries, conduct exam revision sessions ·         Marking a share of project assessment submissions ·         Managing student submissions (e.g. chasing up missing submissions, handling late submissions, handling remark requests, assessment feedback)]   In addition, you will be supported to participate as an academic member of the Faculty in contributing to the Faculty’s academic mission, life and community and supported to engage in at least 30 hours of paid professional development.   Who We Are Looking For We are seeking applicants enrolled as a University of Melbourne student undertaking a PhD in the Faculty of Architecture, Building and Planning with discipline knowledge in built environment history and theory You will be a passionate educator and have the ability to contribute positively, both individually and as part of a team, to educating, inspiring and supporting students. You will also have excellent written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills and excellent organisational skills with the ability to manage one’s own time effectively, administer multiple tasks simultaneously and meet deadlines. For further information regarding responsibilities and requirements, please refer to the attached PD. Position Description JR-019174 Graduate Research Academic Associate in History and Theory PD.docx JR-019174 Graduate Research Academic Associate in History and Theory PD.pdf This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check. This is essential to guarantee a secure environment for all individuals at the University. In the case the selected candidate doesn't have a valid Working with Children Check, they will be able to apply for the WWCC as part of the initial onboarding procedures."
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville-Victoria/Academic-Tutor---Murrup-Barak_JR-018018": {
      "title": "Academic Tutor - Murrup Barak",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nRole type:   Part-time (minimum 0.2 FTE); Fixed term  Department/ School:   Indigenous Portfolio – Murrup Barak  Salary: $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. (pro rata for part-time) plus 17% super   Make a direct impact on Indigenous student success / share real-world discipline expertise / join a supportive Indigenous-led team  Apply now to use your discipline expertise to empower Indigenous students through tailored, high-impact tutoring.  Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.   The University of Melbourne    We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website.   Your next career opportunity    You will provide focused academic tutoring that strengthens success and confidence for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students.  You will join a collaborative team of tutors, shaping culturally safe, empowering learning experiences across diverse disciplines.  What you will deliver:    Design and deliver weekly discipline-specific tutoring sessions, individually and in groups, to support student learning goals.  Guide students to develop self-directed learning strategies while maintaining strong academic standards and integrity.  Collaborate with fellow tutors to create, refine and share discipline resources and participate in professional development.  Build culturally safe, supportive learning environments and make referrals to the Student Success Team when needed.  Maintain accurate tutoring records, schedules and progress notes, ensuring program systems are used effectively.    You may be a great fit if you:    Hold an undergraduate degree and strong knowledge of your discipline at postgraduate level.  Bring experience supporting or understanding Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students in tertiary learning environments.  Demonstrate awareness of systemic barriers affecting Indigenous students and commitment to culturally safe practice.  Coach learners in complex concepts, adapting explanations for different levels and assessment expectations.  Manage tutoring schedules reliably, using program systems and processes to track attendance and progress.  Collaborate effectively within a tutor team and with Student Success colleagues to support student wellbeing.  For further information please refer to the attached PD.   What we offer you!    We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:   Build future financial security with 17% superannuation  Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being   Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning  Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave.  Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company   Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program   For more information check out our benefits page!    Your new team   You will join Murrup Barak, the Indigenous student services hub within the University’s Indigenous Portfolio.  The team collaborates across the University to provide safe, empowering support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students.  You will be part of a growing tutor cohort, valued for discipline expertise, cultural respect and student-centred practice.  Be Yourself    The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb.   We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process.   Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants    We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.        Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au .   Application essentials:   Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs.  You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role.   A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you.   Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process.   You are not required to address the Selection Criteria for this application.   Want to know more?   For queries related to this specific position, contact Josh Cubillo at josh.cubillo@unimelb.edu.au For questions related to our recruitment process email Tara at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au   For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments').   Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future.                                            Applications close: Tuesday 27th October11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.   Position Description:   JR-018018 Academic Tutor - Murrup Barak - PD.pdf   JR-018018 Academic Tutor - Murrup Barak - PD.docx"
    },
    "https://unimelb.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/UoM_External_Career/job/Parkville/Research-Fellow-in-Mathematics_JR-018338": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Mathematics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nRole type: Full-time; Fixed term (2 years) Faculty: Science School : School of Mathematics and Statistics Salary: Level A: AUD $87,266 - $118,416 p.a. plus 17% super ARC-funded Langlands research role / strong emphasis on high-quality mathematical publications / collaborative, internationally connected research environment Apply now to drive cutting-edge research in representation theory and build a strong independent research profile. Investing in you – 17% superannuation, benefits package including salary packaging, health and wellbeing programs, discounted services, and professional development opportunities.  The University of Melbourne   We take pride in our people, who all contribute to our mission to benefit society through the transformative impact of education and research. Discover more via our website. Your next career opportunity   You will contribute to an ARC-funded project at the forefront of the Langlands program. You will develop your own research agenda in algebraic geometry and representation theory within a supportive team. You will gain mentoring, collaboration opportunities and a strong platform for your future academic career. What you will deliver:   Conduct internationally competitive research on representation theory and Langlands-related topics, leading to high quality journal publications. Collaborate closely with Chief Investigators to achieve ARC Discovery Project milestones and contribute to team research outputs. Present research at seminars and conferences, and engage with broader mathematical communities locally and internationally. Contribute to supervision and mentoring of postgraduate and research students in your area of expertise. Assist with preparing funding proposals, managing project administration and meeting reporting requirements for the research program. You may be a great fit if you:   Hold a completed or submitted PhD in mathematics. Demonstrate strong expertise in algebraic geometry and representation theory. Bring experience assisting with supervision of undergraduate or higher degree research projects. Show capacity to design, manage and complete research projects with limited supervision. Communicate research clearly through high-quality written work and effective presentations in English. Value collaborative, inclusive teamwork and thrive in diverse, international mathematical environments. For further information please refer to the attached PD.  What we offer you!   We offer the opportunity to be part of a vibrant community and enjoy a comprehensive range of benefits to support your success and sense of fulfilment, including:  Build future financial security with 17% superannuation Supportive flexible work arrangements underpinned by our commitment to inclusion and well-being  Career development opportunities, including access to a range of tailored programs, such as Academic Women in Leadership, and learning platforms including LinkedIn Learning Progressive, considerate leave provisions to empower your work-life balance and well-being, including leading parental leave, gender affirmation leave, cultural leave, and menstruation and menopause leave. Salary packaging and access to a range of discounted services including Bupa health insurance, and access to unique cultural and recreational benefits such as discounts for the Melbourne Theatre Company  Health and well-being services including a leading Employee Assistance Program  For more information check out our benefits page!  Your new team – School of Mathematics and Statistics   The School of Mathematics and Statistics is one of Australia’s leading centres for mathematical research and teaching. It hosts major ARC centres and fellowships, creating a vibrant hub for advanced work in algebraic geometry and representation theory. Within the Faculty of Science, the School champions curiosity, excellence, impact and integrity, supported by strong global collaborations and a diverse, inclusive community. Be Yourself   The University of Melbourne is an Equal Opportunity Employer and a child-safe organisation. The diversity of our community enriches us all, and we are committed to creating an inclusive and fair workplace where everyone is valued, respected, and empowered to succeed. We welcome applicants from all backgrounds, identities, and experiences. Discover more about Diversity and Inclusion at UniMelb. We're committed to a barrier-free recruitment process and ongoing workplace support, providing adjustments throughout. We warmly encourage applications from people with disabilities. Learn more about how we support an accessible recruitment process. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Applicants   We aspire to be the University of choice for Indigenous Australians. Visit our Indigenous staff page to learn more about our investment and support for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff.    Indigenous applicants are encouraged to connect with our Indigenous Employment & Development team at korin-korin@unimelb.edu.au . Application essentials: Visit \"apply with us\" to learn more about the process, including tips and FAQs. You must have current valid work rights for Australia. Visa sponsorship is not available for this role. A Working with Children Check is required for all positions. If successful, we will guide you through the process and reimburse you. Please upload your resume and a cover letter outlining your interest and experience as part of the application process. Please upload your responses to the Selection Criteria, found in the Position Description. Learn more about responding to Selection Criteria Want to know more? For queries related to this specific position, contact Ting Xue (details in position description) For questions related to our recruitment process email Lachlan Bryce at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au For recruitment adjustments contact Kim Groizard on +61 3 9035 3218 or at hr-careers@unimelb.edu.au (subject: 'Recruitment Adjustments'). Apply today, and join a community that’s shaping the future. Applications close: 1 November, 11:55 PM; Melbourne time zone.  Position Description:   JR-018338 Research Fellow in Mathematics_PD.pdf JR-018338 Research Fellow in Mathematics_PD.docx"
    }
  },
